    </header>
    <body>
        <segment id="1" parent="21" relname="span">Seffah, Kecceci and Donyaee present a very interesting paper on an engineering software model that integrates human computer interface principles into its design.</segment>
        <segment id="2" parent="3" relname="concession">The authors convey that HCI has developed good models for specifying or measuring usability</segment>
        <segment id="3" parent="32" relname="span">but these models are weak in the integration within the software engineering models.</segment>
        <segment id="4" parent="36" relname="span">The authors are suggesting that a relationship should exist between characteristics of software and their measurable attributes. (Seffah, 2001)</segment>
        <segment id="5" parent="31" relname="span">The authors of this paper propose a model called Quality in Use Integrated Map (QUIM).</segment>
        <segment id="6" parent="34" relname="span">QUIM defines quality of usage in relationship to end users perspective of "did this software produce a quality product".</segment>
        <segment id="7" parent="6" relname="elaboration">It is clearly asking "what have you done for me?"</segment>
        <segment id="8" parent="35" relname="span">QUIM is an open model</segment>
        <segment id="9" parent="8" relname="volitional-cause">because it considers cause/effect relationships between the user and developer.</segment>
        <segment id="10" parent="22" relname="span">In conclusion, the paper outlines the similarities and differences, and limitations of a variety models used for measuring usability within HCI and engineering.</segment>
        <segment id="11" parent="10" relname="solutionhood">They suggest QUIM as a solution.</segment>
        <segment id="12" parent="29" relname="span">The intent is to present a simple, low risk and cost effective model that can be used to measure usability.</segment>
        <segment id="13" parent="24" relname="span">This paper goes into quite of bit detail on the QUIM model.</segment>
        <segment id="14" parent="13" relname="elaboration">It presents a rational for the model and outlines the methodology and structure.</segment>
        <segment id="15" parent="28" relname="span">The thing that I found interesting about QUIM is the integration of HCI and engineering.</segment>
        <segment id="16" parent="26" relname="concession">There can be many models of HCI applied to a software project</segment>
        <segment id="17" parent="26" relname="span">but without the integration of engineering into these exercises the outcomes will be limited to partial success.</segment>
        <segment id="18" parent="27" relname="span">As a rule engineers do not interact a great deal wit users.</segment>
        <segment id="19" parent="18" relname="solutionhood">This would certainly facilitate some very productive interaction.</segment>
